## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level synchronization and memory-order reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Ticket-based spinlock yields FIFO fairness
- Atomics: fetch_add, load, store
- Memory orders: acquire on lock, release on unlock, relaxed during waiting

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  _Atomic uint32_t next;
  _Atomic uint32_t serve;
} TicketLock;
void init(TicketLock* t){ atomic_store(&t->next,0); atomic_store(&t->serve,0); }
uint32_t lock(TicketLock* t){ uint32_t my = atomic_fetch_add(&t->next,1); while (atomic_load(&t->serve) != my) { _mm_pause(); } return my; }
void unlock(TicketLock* t, uint32_t my){ atomic_store_explicit(&t->serve, my+1, memory_order_release); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for NUMA or many threads?
- What are practical drawbacks of busy-waiting and how would you verify FIFO fairness under stress?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about crash safety, non-volatile memory semantics, and low-level ordering. Requires explicit use of durable writes, recovery procedures, and a practical benchmark plan.

## Key Concepts

- Crash-consistency and redo-logs
- Durable writes: clwb/flushopt, sfence
- Per-slot sequencing and tail/head management
- Recovery replay and correctness guarantees

## Code Example

```pseudo
structure Slot { payload; header; seq; }
function Enqueue(buf, item): void {
  Slot s = select_next_slot();
  s.payload = item;
  s.header = make_header(++tail_seq);
  persist(s.header); // clwb/flushopt + sfence
  fence();
  tail = tail + 1; // release store
}
function Dequeue(): Item {
  if (head == tail) return EMPTY;
  Item it = read_slot(head);
  head = head + 1; // release
  persist(head);
  return it;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this across NUMA nodes?
- How would you test resilience against partial writes or power loss during enqueue?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical memory management, lock-free data structures, and safe object reuse under contention. A beginner-friendly allocator emphasizes alignment and simple reclaim strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free freelist using CAS
- ABA avoidance (tagged pointers or hazard pointers)
- Slab-style pool with 64-byte blocks
- Cache-line awareness and fragmentation

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code for allocate/free
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;
typedef struct Pool { Block* head; void* pool; size_t block_size; } Pool;

Block* allocate(Pool* p){
  Block* old = atomic_load(&p->head);
  while (old){
    Block* next = old->next;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->head, &old, next)){
      return old;
    }
  }
  return NULL;
}
void deallocate(Pool* p, Block* b){
  Block* head = atomic_load(&p->head);
  do {
    b->next = head;
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->head, &head, b));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect pool exhaustion and recover?
- How would you extend to variable-size objects or per-object headers?

