## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of memory layout, overlap handling, and cache-friendly copy strategies in tight, real-world paths.

## Key Concepts
- Overlap-safe memmove
- Cache-friendly 64-byte blocks
- Alignment assumptions and tail handling
- Fallback to memcpy for tiny or unaligned data
- Microbenchmark plan to measure throughput vs standard memcpy

## Code Example
```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

void *memmove64(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n){
  unsigned char *d = (unsigned char*)dst;
  const unsigned char *s = (const unsigned char*)src;
  const size_t BLOCK = 64;

  if ((size_t)(d) <= (size_t)(s) || d >= s + n){
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      d += BLOCK; s += BLOCK; n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d, s, n);
  } else {
    d += n; s += n;
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      d -= BLOCK; s -= BLOCK;
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d - n, s - n, n);
  }
  return dst;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for arbitrary alignment and larger data sizes?
- Compare performance to builtin memmove on x86-64 with different runtimes.
- What edge cases or architectures could break this approach?

